NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:07.180
Other than convolutional layers, confidence often also use pooling layers to reduce the size of the representation,

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:12.180
the speed of computation, as well as make some of the features it detects a bit more robust.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.180
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:20.180
Let's go through an example of pooling, and then we'll talk about why you might want to do this.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:29.180
Suppose you have a 4x4 input, and you want to apply a type of pooling called max-pooling.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:35.180
And the output of this particular implementation of max-pooling will be a 2x2 output.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.180
And the way you do that is quite simple.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:44.180
Take your 4x4 input and break it into different regions, and I'm going to color the four regions as follows.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:53.180
And then in the output, which is 2x2, each of the outputs will just be the max from the correspondingly shaded region.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:58.180
So the upper left, I guess the max of these four numbers, is 9.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.180
On the upper right, the max of the blue numbers is 2.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.180
Lower left, the biggest number is 6.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.180
And lower right, the biggest number is 3.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:13.180
So to compute each of the numbers on the right, we took the max over a 2x2 region.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:21.180
So this is as if you're applying a filter size of 2, because you're taking a 2x2 region,

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.180
and you're taking a stride of 2.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:32.620
So these are actually the hyperparameters of max-pooling.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:39.620
Because we start from this filter size, it's like a 2x2 region that gives you the 9,

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:45.620
and then you step it over two steps to look at this region to give you the 2.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.620
And then for the next row, you step it down two steps to give it a 6,

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.620
and then step it to the right by two steps to give you 3.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.620
So because the squares are 2x2, f is equal to 2,

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:00.620
and because you stride by 2, s is equal to 2.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.620
So here's the intuition behind what max-pooling is doing.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:09.620
If you think of this 4x4 input as some set of features,

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:15.620
maybe not...if you think of this 4x4 region as some set of features,

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.620
the activations in some layer of the neural network,

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:22.620
then a large number means that it's maybe detected a particular feature.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:26.620
So the upper left-hand quadrant has this particular feature,

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:30.620
maybe a vertical edge, or maybe an eye, or a whisker,

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:31.620
which kind of detect the cat.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.620
But clearly that feature exists in the upper left-hand quadrant,

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:39.620
whereas this feature maybe doesn't cat eye detector,

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:43.620
whereas this feature doesn't really exist in the upper right-hand quadrant.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.620
So what the max operation does is so long as the feature is detected anywhere

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:52.620
in one of these quadrants, it then remains preserved in the output of max-pooling.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.620
So what the max operator does is really say,

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.620
if this feature is detected anywhere in this filter, then keep a high number.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:03.620
But if this feature is not detected,

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:07.620
so maybe if this feature doesn't exist in the upper right-hand quadrant,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.620
then the max of all those numbers is still itself quite small.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.620
So maybe that's the intuition behind max-pooling.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:19.620
But I have to admit, I think the main reason people use max-pooling is because

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:23.620
it's been found in a lot of experiments to work well,

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:26.620
and the intuition I just described, despite it being often cited,

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:32.620
I don't know if anyone fully knows if that's the real underlying reason.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.620
I don't know if anyone knows if that's the real underlying reason

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:39.620
that max-pooling works well in confidence.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.620
One interesting property of max-pooling is that

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:47.620
it has a set of hyperparameters, but it has no parameters to learn.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.620
There's actually nothing for gradient descent to learn.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:53.620
Once you fix f and s, it's just a fixed computation,

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:56.620
and gradient descent doesn't change anything.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:00.620
Let's go through an example with some different hyperparameters.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:04.620
Here you have a 5x5 input,

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:09.620
and we're going to apply max-pooling with a filter size that's 3x3,

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:13.620
so f is equal to 3, and let's use a stride of 1.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:17.620
In this case, the output size is going to be 3x3.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.620
The formulas we had developed in the previous videos for figuring out

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:26.620
the output size for a conned layer, those formulas also work for max-pooling.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:33.620
So that n plus 2p minus f over s plus 1,

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:37.620
that formula also works for figuring out the output size of max-pool.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:42.620
But in this example, let's compute each of the elements of this 3x3 output.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:46.620
The upper left hand elements, we're going to look over that region.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.620
Notice this is a 3x3 region because the filter size is 3,

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:53.620
and the max is there, so that's going to be 9.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:57.620
And then we'll shift it over by 1 because we're taking a stride of 1.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:01.620
So that max in the blue box is 9.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:05.620
Let's shift that over again. The max in the blue box is 5.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:09.620
And then let's go on to the next row, a stride of 1.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:12.620
So we're just stepping down by one step.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.620
So max in that region is 9.

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:17.620
Max in that region is 9.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:19.620
Max in that region...

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.620
Oh, there are two 5s, but the max is a 5.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.620
And then finally, max in that is 8.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.620
Max in that is 6.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.620
And max in that is 9 in the lower right hand corner.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:38.620
So this with this set of hyper-ramps, this f equals 3, s equals 1, gives that output shown on it.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:44.620
Now, so far I've shown max pooling on a 2D input.

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:52.620
If you have a 3D input, then the output will have the same dimension.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:58.620
So for example, if you have 5x5x2, then the output will be 3x3x2.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:08.120
And the way you compute max pooling is you perform the computation we just described on each of the channels independently.

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:12.120
So the first channel, which is shown here on top, is still the same.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:16.120
And then for the second channel, I guess this one that I just drew at the bottom,

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:20.120
you would do the same computation on that slice of this volume.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:24.120
And that gives you this second slice.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:29.120
And more generally, if this was 5x5 by some number of channels,

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:33.120
the output would be 3x3 by that same number of channels.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:41.660
And the max pooling computation is done independently on each of these NC channels.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:45.860
So that's max pooling.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:50.860
There's one other type of pooling that isn't used very often, but I'll mention briefly,

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:52.860
which is average pooling.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:58.860
So it does pretty much what you'd expect, which is instead of taking the maxes

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.860
within each filter, you take the average.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:07.860
So in this example, the average of the numbers in purple is 3.75,

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:12.860
then that's 1.25, and 4, and 2.

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:17.860
And so this is average pooling with hyperparameters f equals 2, s equals 2.

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:21.860
We can choose other hyperparameters as well.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:26.860
So these days, max pooling is used much more often than average pooling,

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:31.860
with one exception, which is sometimes very deep in the neural network.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:39.860
You might use average pooling to collapse your representation from, say, 7x7 by 1000,

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:44.860
and average over all the spatial extents to get 1x1 by 1000.

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:47.860
We'll see an example of this later.

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:53.860
But you see max pooling used much more in the neural network than average pooling.

00:07:53.860 --> 00:08:02.860
So just to summarize, the hyperparameters for pooling are f, the filter size, and s, the stride.

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:07.860
And maybe common choices of parameters might be f equals 2, s equals 2.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:13.860
This is used quite often, and this has the effect of roughly shrinking the height and width

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:15.860
by a factor of about 2.

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:20.860
And the common choice of hyperparameters might be f equals 2, s equals 2,

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:27.860
and this has the effect of shrinking the height and width of the representation by a factor of 2.

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:31.860
I've also seen f equals 3, s equals 2 used.

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:36.860
And then the other hyperparameter is just the binary bit that says,

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:40.860
are you using max pooling or are you using average pooling?

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:45.860
If you want, you can add an extra hyperparameter for the padding,

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:47.860
although this is very, very rarely used.

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:50.860
When you do max pooling, usually you do not use any padding,

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:54.860
although there is one exception that we'll see next week as well.

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:58.860
But for the most part, max pooling usually does not use any padding.

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:06.180
So the most common value of p by far is p equals 0.

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:12.180
And the input of max pooling is that you input a volume of size that,

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:21.180
nh by nw by nc, and it would output a volume of size given by this.

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:32.180
So assuming there's no padding by nw minus f over s, s1, 4 by nc.

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:35.180
And so the number of input channels is equal to the number of output channels

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:41.180
because pooling applies to each of your channels independently.

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:47.180
One thing to note about pooling is that there are no parameters to learn.

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:52.180
So when you implement backprop, you find that there are no parameters

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:55.180
that backprop will adapt through max pooling.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:58.180
Instead, there are just these hyperparameters that you set once,

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:01.180
maybe set once by hand or set using cross-validation.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:03.180
And then beyond that, you're done.

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:07.180
And it's just a fixed function that the neural network computes in one of the layers,

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:09.180
and there is actually nothing to learn.

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:11.180
It's just a fixed function.

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:13.180
So that's it for pooling.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:16.180
You now know how to build convolutional layers and pooling layers.

